<script>
  import Aside from '$lib/components/Aside.svelte';
  import Screenshot from '$lib/components/Screenshot.svelte';
</script>

Last week we built a database explorer — a tool for readers to dig through records and find their own story. This week we're flipping the script. Instead of letting the reader drive, we're going to tell the story ourselves, using photographs.

We're going to build a photo essay about Harlem in 1943, as seen through the camera of Gordon Parks. And we're going to design it for the way people actually look at photos today: on their phones, one image at a time, swiping through.

## Part 1: Gordon Parks and the idea

[Gordon Parks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gordon_Parks) (1912–2006) was the first Black photographer hired by the Farm Security Administration, the federal agency that documented American life during the Great Depression and World War II. He called his camera "a weapon against poverty and racism."

In the spring and summer of 1943, Parks photographed daily life in Harlem for the Office of War Information. He shot portraits of residents, newsboys, street scenes, children, families, and the social fabric of the neighborhood. The images are intimate, dignified, and alive.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-10/parks-harlem-overview.png"
  alt="A grid of Gordon Parks photographs from the Library of Congress showing Harlem street scenes and portraits from 1943"
  chromeTitle="Library of Congress"
  chromeUrl="https://www.loc.gov/pictures/search/?q=gordon+parks+harlem&st=grid"
  maxWidth="960px"
/>

Because the work was produced for the federal government, every image is in the public domain. The Library of Congress holds the original negatives and offers high-resolution scans, free to download, free to publish, free to use for any purpose.

We're going to curate a selection of these photographs and build them into a visual story. But we're not going to make a traditional web page with text paragraphs and images stacked in a column. We're going to make something that feels native to a phone.

### Designing for mobile first

Think about how you browse photos on your phone. Not on a website. On Instagram. On TikTok. On Reels. You see one image at a time. It fills the entire screen. You swipe to advance. The text is overlaid on the image, not sitting next to it. The whole experience is vertical, immersive, and fast.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-10/reels-example.png"
  alt="A smartphone mockup showing the vertical, full-screen photo layout of Instagram Reels with overlaid text at the bottom of the screen"
  chromeTitle="Instagram"
  chromeUrl="https://www.instagram.com/reels/"
  maxWidth="400px"
/>

This week we're going to steal that design pattern for journalism. Each of Parks's photographs will fill the entire screen. Captions overlay the bottom of the image. Scrolling snaps from one photo to the next with a satisfying click. On a phone, it will feel like swiping through a reel. On a desktop, it will still work — just with the scroll wheel instead of a thumb.

The key CSS feature that makes this possible is called [scroll snap](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/CSS_scroll_snap). It's a set of properties that tell the browser to "snap" the scroll position to specific points on the page, rather than letting the user stop at any arbitrary position. No JavaScript required.

Let's build it.

### Set up your project

Open your browser and navigate to our template repository at <a href="https://github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template" target="_blank">github.com/palewire/cuny-jour-static-site-template</a>. Click "Use this template" and create a new repository called `parks-harlem-1943`. Clone it, open it in VS Code, and run:

```bash
npm install
```

Then:

```bash
npm run dev
```

## Part 2: Setting up the photos

I've prepared a set of Gordon Parks photographs for today's class. Download the zip file from the link in the class chat. Inside you'll find two things:

1. A folder of JPEG images — 15 photographs I've curated from the Library of Congress
2. A file called `photos.json` with metadata for each image

Unzip the folder and copy the images into `static/photos/` in your project. You'll need to create the `photos` folder inside `static/` first.

Then copy `photos.json` to `src/lib/data/photos.json`. Create the `data` folder inside `src/lib/` if it doesn't exist from last week.

Your project structure should now look like this:

```
static/
  photos/
    parks-harlem-01.jpg
    parks-harlem-02.jpg
    parks-harlem-03.jpg
    ...
src/
  lib/
    data/
      photos.json
```

Open `photos.json` and take a look:

```json
[
  {
    "id": 1,
    "filename": "parks-harlem-01.jpg",
    "title": "A Harlem newsboy",
    "date": "1943",
    "caption": "A young newsboy on the streets of Harlem. Gordon Parks photographed the children of the neighborhood with a warmth that conveyed their individuality and dignity.",
    "credit": "Gordon Parks / FSA–OWI / Library of Congress"
  },
  {
    "id": 2,
    "filename": "parks-harlem-02.jpg",
    "title": "A woman and her dog in the Harlem section",
    "date": "1943",
    "caption": "Parks documented the residents of Harlem as they went about their daily lives, often capturing quiet moments of connection.",
    "credit": "Gordon Parks / FSA–OWI / Library of Congress"
  }
]
```

Each photo has an `id`, the `filename` that matches the image in `static/photos/`, a `title` from the original Library of Congress caption card, a `caption` I've written to provide context, and a `credit` line.

## Part 3: The first slide

Let's start by getting a single photo to fill the entire screen. Clear out the default content in `src/routes/+page.svelte` and replace it with:

```svelte
<script>
  import photos from '$lib/data/photos.json';
</script>

<div class="gallery">
  <div class="slide">
    <img
      src="/photos/{photos[0].filename}"
      alt={photos[0].title}
    />
  </div>
</div>
```

Save and check your browser. You'll see the image, but it's just sitting on the page at its natural size, surrounded by the template's default padding and margins.

We need to break out of the template's layout and take over the entire viewport. Add a style block at the bottom of the file:

```css
:global(body) {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
}

.gallery {
  height: 100dvh;
  overflow-y: scroll;
}

.slide {
  height: 100dvh;
  position: relative;
  background: black;
}

.slide img {
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  object-fit: cover;
}
```

There are several important things happening here. Let's walk through them.

The [`:global(body)`](https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/global-styles) rule overrides the default body styles from our template. The `:global()` wrapper tells Svelte to apply this style globally rather than scoping it to this component. We're removing all margins and padding and setting `overflow: hidden` so only our gallery scrolls, not the page itself.

The `.gallery` container takes up the full height of the viewport using [`100dvh`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/length#dynamic_viewport_units). The `dvh` unit stands for "dynamic viewport height." Unlike the older `vh` unit, it accounts for browser toolbars that appear and disappear on mobile phones. When you scroll down on your phone and the address bar hides itself, `100dvh` expands to fill the newly available space. This is essential for a full-screen mobile experience.

Each `.slide` also fills exactly `100dvh`, so one slide equals one screen.

The `img` tag is set to fill its parent with `width: 100%` and `height: 100%`. But what about photos that aren't the same shape as the screen? That's where [`object-fit: cover`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/object-fit) comes in. It tells the browser to scale the image up until it completely fills the container, cropping whatever doesn't fit rather than squishing or stretching. The result is a full-bleed photo with no letterboxing or empty space.

Save and check your browser. The first photo should now fill the entire screen, edge to edge, with the excess cropped away.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-10/first-slide-fullscreen.png"
  alt="A single Gordon Parks photograph filling the entire browser viewport with no margins, padding, or chrome visible"
  chromeTitle="Parks Harlem 1943"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="400px"
/>

Try resizing your browser window. The image adapts smoothly to any shape — tall, wide, square. That's the power of `object-fit: cover`.

## Part 4: All the slides, with scroll snap

Now let's show all the photos. Replace the single slide with a loop:

```svelte
<div class="gallery">
  {#each photos as photo}
    <div class="slide">
      <img
        src="/photos/{photo.filename}"
        alt={photo.title}
      />
    </div>
  {/each}
</div>
```

Save and scroll through the page. You'll see all 15 photos, each filling the screen. But the scrolling is free — you can stop anywhere, halfway between two photos, which looks bad.

This is where scroll snap comes in. Add two CSS properties:

```css
.gallery {
  height: 100dvh;
  overflow-y: scroll;
  scroll-snap-type: y mandatory;
}

.slide {
  height: 100dvh;
  position: relative;
  background: black;
  scroll-snap-align: start;
}
```

Two new lines, and the whole experience transforms.

[`scroll-snap-type: y mandatory`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/scroll-snap-type) on the gallery container tells the browser: "When the user stops scrolling on the vertical axis, snap to the nearest snap point. And make it mandatory — always snap, no exceptions."

[`scroll-snap-align: start`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/scroll-snap-align) on each slide says: "My snap point is my top edge." So when snapping happens, the top of a slide aligns with the top of the viewport.

Save and try scrolling now. Each swipe or scroll click locks precisely to the next photo. On a phone, this will feel like swiping through Instagram.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-10/scroll-snap-working.png"
  alt="Two side-by-side screenshots showing the gallery mid-scroll and then snapped cleanly to a photo, demonstrating the scroll-snap behavior"
  chromeTitle="Parks Harlem 1943"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
  maxWidth="600px"
/>

This is one of those moments where a very small amount of CSS creates a dramatic change in the user experience. Two properties, zero JavaScript, and the whole page feels like a native app.

## Part 5: Caption overlays

The photos fill the screen, but the reader has no context. Let's overlay captions on each image, the way Reels and Stories put text at the bottom of the frame.

Update the slide markup:

```svelte
<div class="gallery">
  {#each photos as photo}
    <div class="slide">
      <img
        src="/photos/{photo.filename}"
        alt={photo.title}
      />
      <div class="caption">
        <h2>{photo.title}</h2>
        <p>{photo.caption}</p>
        <span class="credit">{photo.credit}</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  {/each}
</div>
```

Now add styles for the caption overlay:

```css
.caption {
  position: absolute;
  bottom: 0;
  left: 0;
  right: 0;
  padding: 2rem 1.5rem;
  background: linear-gradient(
    to top,
    rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.85) 0%,
    rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4) 70%,
    transparent 100%
  );
  color: white;
}

.caption h2 {
  margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  font-size: 1.25rem;
  line-height: 1.3;
}

.caption p {
  margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  font-size: 0.9375rem;
  line-height: 1.5;
  opacity: 0.9;
}

.caption .credit {
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  opacity: 0.6;
}
```

The key trick here is [`position: absolute`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/position) on the caption combined with `position: relative` on the slide (which we already set). This pulls the caption out of the normal document flow and positions it relative to the slide container. Setting `bottom: 0` pins it to the bottom edge.

The [`linear-gradient`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/gradient/linear-gradient) background creates a fade from near-black at the bottom to transparent at the top. This ensures the white text is readable regardless of what's in the photo behind it. It's the same technique used by Instagram, YouTube, and every streaming service's thumbnail overlays.

Save and scroll through. Each photo now has its title, caption, and credit floating over the bottom of the image.

