## Part 8: A slide counter

As readers scroll through, it helps to know where they are. Let's add a small counter in the corner — something like "3 / 15" — that updates as they move through the gallery.

This is a perfect use of `$state`. We need to track which slide is currently visible and update a number on screen.

We'll use a browser feature called [`IntersectionObserver`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Intersection_Observer_API) to detect which slide is on screen. Don't worry about memorizing how it works — it's the kind of thing you look up when you need it. The important thing is what it does: it watches a set of elements and tells you when they enter or leave the viewport.

Update the script block in `+page.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  import { onMount } from 'svelte';
  import photos from '$lib/data/photos.json';
  import TitleSlide from '$lib/components/TitleSlide.svelte';
  import PhotoSlide from '$lib/components/PhotoSlide.svelte';

  let currentSlide = $state(0);
  let galleryEl;

  onMount(() => {
    const slides = galleryEl.querySelectorAll('[data-slide-index]');

    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries) => {
        entries.forEach((entry) => {
          if (entry.isIntersecting) {
            currentSlide = parseInt(entry.target.dataset.slideIndex);
          }
        });
      },
      {
        root: galleryEl,
        threshold: 0.5,
      }
    );

    slides.forEach((slide) => observer.observe(slide));

    return () => observer.disconnect();
  });
</script>
```

There's a lot happening here, so let's break it down.

`currentSlide` is a `$state` variable that starts at `0` and tracks which photo is on screen.

`galleryEl` is a variable that will hold a reference to the gallery container. We need it so we can tell the observer which element is the scroll container.

[`onMount`](https://svelte.dev/docs/svelte/lifecycle-hooks#onMount) is a Svelte function that runs code after the page has loaded and all the HTML elements exist. We need this because we can't observe elements that haven't been created yet.

Inside `onMount`, we find all elements with a `data-slide-index` attribute (we'll add those in a moment), create an `IntersectionObserver` that fires whenever a slide is at least 50% visible (`threshold: 0.5`), and update `currentSlide` to that slide's index.

The `return () => observer.disconnect()` at the end is cleanup — it tells Svelte to stop observing when the component is destroyed, which prevents memory leaks.

Now update the markup to wire everything together:

```svelte
<div class="gallery" bind:this={galleryEl}>
  <TitleSlide
    headline="Harlem, 1943"
    intro="In the spring of 1943, Gordon Parks photographed daily life in Harlem for the federal government. His camera found dignity in the everyday — newsboys, neighbors, children, and the streets they shared. These images, now in the public domain, are a window into a neighborhood and a moment in time."
    byline="Photographs by Gordon Parks · Farm Security Administration–Office of War Information"
  />

  {#each photos as photo, i}
    <div data-slide-index={i + 1}>
      <PhotoSlide {photo} />
    </div>
  {/each}
</div>

{#if currentSlide > 0}
  <div class="counter">
    {currentSlide} / {photos.length}
  </div>
{/if}
```

Three changes to notice.

First, `bind:this={galleryEl}` on the gallery div gives us a reference to the actual HTML element, which we pass to the `IntersectionObserver` as its `root`.

Second, the `{#each}` now includes `, i` after `photo`. That second variable gives us the index of each item in the loop, starting at 0. We wrap each `PhotoSlide` in a div with `data-slide-index={i + 1}` so the first photo is slide 1, the second is slide 2, and so on.

Third, the counter itself only appears when `currentSlide > 0` — it's hidden on the title slide and shows once you've scrolled into the photos.

Add styles for the counter:

```css
.counter {
  position: fixed;
  top: 1rem;
  right: 1rem;
  background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.6);
  color: white;
  padding: 0.25rem 0.75rem;
  border-radius: 1rem;
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  z-index: 10;
}
```

The counter is `position: fixed` so it stays in the top-right corner as you scroll. The [`font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/font-variant-numeric) property makes digits fixed-width, so the counter doesn't jump around when numbers change from "1" to "10."

Save and scroll through. The counter should appear after the title slide and update with each snap.

