## Part 9: A credits slide

Just as we opened with a title, let's close with a credits slide that tells the reader more about where these photographs came from.

Create `src/lib/components/CreditsSlide.svelte`:

```svelte
<script>
  let { children } = $props();
</script>

<div class="slide">
  <div class="content">
    {@render children()}
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  .slide {
    height: 100dvh;
    position: relative;
    background: black;
    scroll-snap-align: start;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }

  .content {
    max-width: 600px;
    padding: 2rem;
    color: white;
  }

  .content :global(h2) {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .content :global(p) {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    opacity: 0.85;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
  }

  .content :global(a) {
    color: white;
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  .content :global(a:hover) {
    opacity: 1;
  }
</style>
```

This component uses the `children` pattern from [Week 3](/docs/coding-the-news/scripts/week-3/#part-3-creating-a-parent-component) so we can write free-form HTML inside the component tags. The `:global()` selectors inside the scoped styles target elements that are passed in as children.

Import it and add it at the end of the gallery in `+page.svelte`:

```js
import CreditsSlide from '$lib/components/CreditsSlide.svelte';
```

```svelte
  <CreditsSlide>
    <h2>About These Photographs</h2>
    <p>
      Gordon Parks (1912–2006) was a self-taught photographer, filmmaker,
      writer, and composer. In 1942, he became the first Black photographer
      hired by the Farm Security Administration. His work for the FSA and
      its successor, the Office of War Information, is held by the Library
      of Congress and is in the public domain.
    </p>
    <p>
      The photographs in this essay were taken in Harlem, New York, in
      the spring and summer of 1943. High-resolution scans of the original
      negatives are available for free download from the
      <a href="https://www.loc.gov/pictures/search/?q=gordon+parks+harlem" target="_blank">
        Library of Congress Prints &amp; Photographs Division</a>.
    </p>
  </CreditsSlide>
</div>
```

Place this right before the closing `</div>` of the gallery, after the `{#each}` block.

Save and scroll to the end. After the last photograph, you'll land on a clean black screen with the story behind the images.

